µm 
1,2 $ Basidiocarps small, marasmioid; stipe insititious, filiform or not, glabrous, pruinose, finely pubescent or hairy
1,6 $ Basidiocarps larger, collybioid, rarely tricholomatoid; stipe never insititious, never filiform, glabrous, pubescent or fibrillose; cheilocystidia never in the form of broom cells; pileipellis never hymeniform also in primordial stage, without broom cells
2,3 $  (1)  Stipe filiform, glabrous, pruinose or finely pubescent; cheilocystidia in the form of broom cells or coralloid elements; pileipellis hymeniform in primordial stages, soon an irregular trichoderm, often mixed with single broom cells
2,4 $ Stipe filiform or not, pubescent to hairy, dark coloured, especielly in lower part; context often with unpleasant smell; rhizomorphs absent; pileipellis an ixocutis consisting of cylindrical hyphae without Ramealis-structure; cheilocystidia absent or scattered and indistinct
3,0 $  (2) Stipe blackish brown, glabrous, shilling; pileus chocolate-brown or dark pinkish brown with pinkish yellow to pale pink margin; lamellae incarnate to pinkish brown; on sticks, needles and leaves of coniferous and deciduous trees and shrubs (if smell of rotten cabbage and stipe hairy, cf. Gymnopus perforons) $G. androsaceus
3,0 $ Stipe sordid red-brown, finely hairy, especially when young, glabrescens with âge; pileus pale pink or pinkish brown with pale, almost white margin; lamellae white to pale cream coloured; on dead leaves of Quercus $G. quercophilus
4,0 $  (2) Basidiocarps small, collybioid; pileus 6-8 mm broad, when moist dark yellowish brown; stipe 5 x 1.3-1.5 mm, yellow-brown, very minutely granular or pruinose in same colour; context with indistinct smell; spores 7.0-8.5 x 4.0-5.0 µm ; clamps absent; on bark of a deciduous tree $ G. bisporiger
4,5 $ Basidiocarps larger, marasmioid or collybioid; stipe 15-40 mm long; context with unpleasant smell; clamps present
5,0 $  (4) Basidiocarp omphalioid or collybioid; pileus 8-30 mm broad, red-brown, yellow-brown or brown ochraceous, pallescent to ochraceous red on drying; stipe 15-30 (40) x \-4 mm, cylindrical to tapering towards base, sometimes laterally compressed, entirely pruinose- hairy with dark hairs; caulocystidia scattered, especially in upper part of stipe; on dead branches, sticks, etc. of deciduous trees and scrubs $G. foetidus
5,0 $ Basidiocarps marasmioid; pileus 5-15 (20) mm broad, when moist pinkish brown, pallescent on drying; stipe 15-40 x 0.5-1 mm, filiform, at apex concolorous with pileus, in lower parts dark brown to black, hairy; context with unpleasant, fetid smell like rotten cabbage; caulocystidia abundant; on fallen needles of Picea, rarely also Pinus and Abies $ G. perforans
6,0 $  (1)  Stipe 50-110 (160) x 8-20 mm, red-brown, fusoid or irregularly compressed, strongly fibrillose-costate, tapering towards a deeply rooting base $ G. fusipes
6,7 $ Stipe différent
7,8 $  (6)  Smell strong, unpleasant, reminisccnt of rotten cabbage, sewage, onions or garlic .
7,17 $ Smell indistinct
8,9 $  (7)  Pileus pinkish brown, ochraceous-pink, pale (pinkish) brown, pale red-brown, strongly pallescent on drying; lamellae often rather crowded
8,10 $ Pileus and/or stipe darker; lamellae moderately distant to very distant
9,0 $  (8)  Stipe white at apex, brown below; on humus and coarse litter of Fagus or Betula in deciduous or mixed deciduous-coniferous woods $ G. hariolorum
9,0 $ Stipe dark brown with purplish blue or steel blue tinge; humus and coarse litter of Fagus or Betula in deciduous or mixed deciduous-coniferous woods $G. alpicola
10,11 $  (8)  Stipe ochraceous brown or ochraceous yellowish or slightly orangish, originating from a small sclerotium or not
10,12 $ Stipe darker, orangish-, red-, purple- or black-brown, never originating from a sclerotium
11,0 $  (10)  Pileus dark brown to almost black-brown, dark reddish brown or dirty brown at margin, pallescent to ochraceous brown stipe ochraceous brown, originating from a small sclerotium $ G. graveolens
11,0 $ Pileus rather permanently brown-red, pallescent when diy or slightly cream orange at centre, stipe ochraceous yellowish or slightly orangish, not originating from a sclerotium . . $ G. pyrenaeicus
12,0 $  (10) Cheilocystidia absent $G. herinkii
12,13 $ Cheilocystidia present, well-differentiated
13,0 $  (12) Stipe glabrous, polished, at least in most of its length $ G. erythropus
13,14 $ Stipe pruinose to hairy, at least when young
14,15 $  (13)  Pileus not translucently striate or at margin only; lamellae tinged brown from the beginning; stipe pruinose, glabrescent with âge
14,16 $ Pileus translucently striate at least up to half the radius, glabrous; lamellae white at first, stipe pruinose to hairy, dark when dry
15,0 $  (14)  Pileus dark red-brown when moist, pallescent to pinkish or ochraceous grey-brown on drying; lamellae more crowded (26-34), darker, almost concolorous with pileus; stipe entirely pruinose, then almost smooth and glabrous to slightly fibrillose, uniformly dark brown; spores (7.5) 8.0-11 (12) x (3.0) 3.3-4.5 µm  $ G. dysosmus
15,0 $ Pileus dark brown with pink or reddish tinge, darker at centre, paler at margin, pallescent on drying to sordid ochraceous brown, pinkish brown or greyish brown; lamellae pale cream then pale pinkish or greyish-brownish; stipe lilac, pinkish or sordid brown with paler, pinkish yellow apex, glabrescent with âge; spores (5.5) 6.5-9.0 (9.5) x (2.9) 3.2- 4.5 µm  $G. impudicus
16,0 $  (14) Pileus dark red-brown or yellow-brown, pallescent to incarnate-brown or ochre-brown; on leaves and branch lets of deciduous trees . $ G. brassicolens var. brassicolens
16,0 $ Pileus pale reddish or pinkish brown, pallescent to almost white; on coniferous débris $ G. brassicolens var.pallidus
17,18 $  (7) Stipe at least for most of its length glabrous, polished (apex may be pruinose, base may be tomentose-strigose)
17,29 $ Stipe fibrillose, fibrillose-striate, pruinose, floccose, hairy or tomentose over most of its length
18,19 $  (17) Stipe fairly dark grey-brown or red-brown over its whole length, base often blackish brown
18,21 $ Stipe, at least in most of its length yellow, pale red, yellow-brown, orange-grey or orange- brown, pale brown, reddish brown, except sometimes for the red-brown base
19,0 $  (18) Basidiocarps growing in dense bundles of many specimens together from a common base; pileipellis a cutis or thin ixocutis of rather simple, cylindrical hyphae $ G. acervatus
19,20 $ Basidiocarps growing single, in groups or in small clusters, but then pileipellis with distinct Dryophila-structure
20,0 $  (18) Pileus dark red-brown at centre, much paler yellow-brown to yellow-red towards mar gin; lamellae pale cream-coloured; caulocystidia absent; pileipellis with well-developed Dryophila-structure; hyphae of trama not turning green in alkali; in deciduous woods {Fagus, Quercus), more rarely associated with Picea or Pinus $G. erythropus
20,0 $ Pileus chocolate-brown, paler at outermost margin only; lamellae pale brown to Isabella grey; caulocystidia sparse to numerous; pileipellis with poorly developed Dryophila- structure; hyphae of trama turning green in alkali; in alpine habitat among Leuseleuria procumbens $G. loiseleurietorum
21,22 $  (18) Pileus not translucently striate or at margin only, uniformly pale to dark (reddish) brown
21,26 $ Pileus distinctly translucently striate, pale yellow, yellow, orange-brown, sometimes with darker centre
22,23 $  (21) Spores 7.5-14 x 5.5-7.5 (8.0) µm ; well-developed cheilocystidia absent
22,25 $ Spores smaller; cheilocystidia present
23,0 $  (22) Spores 7.5-10 (11) x 5.5-7.5 (8.0) µm , E = 1.2-1.7, Q = 1.3-1.5, broadly ellipsoid, sub- ovoid, rarely subglobose; basidia mostly 4-spored; pileus weak red to dusky red, with margin pale red; on soil, mossy banks, among fallen, decaying leaves of Quercus ilex ssp. ballota and Pinus pinea $G. castaneus
23,24 $ Spores 9.0-14 x 4.0-5.5 µm , E = 1.8-3.0, Q = 2.0-2.6, pip-shaped or subfusoid; basidia mostly 2-spored; pileus brown, reddish-brown or violaceous-brown; in xerothermic acidophilous stands with Cistus
24,0 $  (23) Spores 9.0-11 x 4.5-5.5 (6.0) µm , E = (1.5) 1.8-2.2 (2.4), Q = 2.0-2.1 ; pileus brown to reddish-brown $ G. bisporus
24,0 $ Spores 10-14 x 4.0-5.0 µm , E = 2.0-3.0, Q = 2.6; pileus reddish-brown to violaceous- brown $G. inusitatus
25,0 $  (22) Spores (4.6) 5.1-6.3 x 2.5-3.5 (4.0) µm ; cheilocystidia 12-45 x 3.0-9.0 µm , irregularly cylindrical or elevate to spheropedunculate, with one or more short, finger-like apical projections; lamellae usually distinctly yellow, rarely white; pileipellis with well-developed Dryophila-structure $G. ocior
25,0 $ Spores (6.2) 6.5-8.5 x 3.0-4.4 pin, cheilocystidia 16.5-30 (44) x 6.0-11.5 µm , clavate to coralloid, often lobed with broad, blunt projections; lamellae white; pileipellis with poorly developed Dryophila-structure $G. alpinus
26,27 $  (21 ) Lamellae pale orange-grey, ochre-brown to brown in mature specimens
26,28 $ Lamellae white, pale cream-coloured to when mature
27,0 $  (26) Pileipellis with distinct Dryophila-structure; cheilocystidia présent, but inconspicuous, 18-26 x (3.1) 5.2-6.6 µm , clavate to cylindrical, irregular; in autumn in thermophilous Quercus forests $G. hybridus
27,0 $ Pileipellis a cutis of inflated terminal elements with some latéral projections, but not forming a real Dryophila-structure; cheilocystidia absent $G. vernus
28,0 $  (26) Cheilocystidia irregularly cylindrical or slightly clavate with one or more coralloid out- growths; pileus with ochre-brown linges, especially at centre, translucently striate up to half the radius; stipe more or less equal $ G. dryophilus var. dryophilus
28,0 $ Cheilocystidia clavate, usually without appendages or outgrowths; pileus pale yellow, usually without ochre or brown tinges, deeply translucently striate, almost to centre; stipe often with distinctly inflated basal part $G. aquosus
29,0 $  ( 17) Basidiocarps growing fasciculate from a common base, few to many together; lamellae remarkably thin and crowded; incrustation never turning olivaceous in alkalis $ G. confluens
29,30 $ Basidiocarps growing single or fasciculate, but then lamellae normally spaced to distant; if lamellae close then incrustation turning green in alkalis
30,0 $  (29) Lamellae remarkable distant, pinkish or yellowish brown; stipe densely woolly-strigose with white to yellow hairs; taste acrid $G. peronatus
30,31 $ Lamellae normally distant to crowded; stipe pruinose, fibrillose to hairy, rarely strigose; taste mild or unpleasantly rancid
31,32 $  (30) Stipe fibrillose lengthwise, not markedly pruinose, hairy, floccose or strigose
31,35 $ Stipe pruinose, pubescent, hairy or strigose
32,0 $  (31 ) Pileus dark chestnut brown, purplish date-brown to sepia; lamellae rather distant (20-30), brownish or umber; stipe robust (30-50 x 3-10 (20) mm); context with unpleasant rancid smell and taste $ G. amygdalisporus
32,33 $ Combination of characters différent; context never with unpleasant rancid smell and taste
33,0 $  (32) Basidiocarps rather robust (pileus 30-110 mm broad) with strongly fibri1lose-striatc stipe and many lamellae (L up to 200 or more); on compost heaps or wood-chips $ G. luxurians
33,34 $ Basidiocarps smaller (pileus up to 50 mm broad) with fewer lamellae; on litter or on soil in grassy places
