42,0 $  (41) Pileus 10-50 mm broad, chestnut brown, chestnut purplish or date brown when young, then rusty tawny, brick or purplish date, uniformly coloured or darker at centre, radially fibrillose (similar to some Inocybe species); stipe 20-50 x 3-8 mm, white to concolorous with pileus; spores 8.0-10 (11) x 4.0-5.0 (6.0) j.im, ellipsoid to lacrimoid; cheilocystidia scattered to rather frequent, cylindrical, clavate, fusoid, or subvesiculose, simple, irregular or with some projections to subcoralloid; pleurocystidia absent or scattered, cylindrical, narrowly fusoid or narrowly clavate elements slightly projecting beyond hyménium; on soil in grassy anthropogenous places $ G. potassiovirescens
42,43 $ Pileus smaller, up to 18 (25) mm, never radially fibrillose; stipe smaller (15-30 x 2-3 mm) or longer (up to 90 mm), purplish brown, mahogany-brown, ochraceous-brown to reddish-brown; spores larger, (7.5) 8.5-14 x 4.0-5.5 jxm, pip-shaped or subfusoid; true cheilocystidia absent or present; in xerothermic acidophilous stands with Cistus or grass on acidic soil in gardens, parkland or heatland végétation
43,0 $  (42) Spores (7.5) 8.5-11 ( 12) x (3.5) 4.0-5.0 µm ; basidia 4-spored; pileus dark purplish brown when young, then mahogany-brown, often with a paler ochraceous-brown margin; stipe very long (up to 90 mm), more-times longer than pileus diameter, of a similar colour to the pileus; true cheilocystidia 21-54 x 5.0-12 (15) µm , fréquent, cylindrical, clavate, (sub)fusoid, subutriform, regular, irregular to subcoralloid, sometimes lobed or with projections), rarely branched or rostrate; amongst short grass on acidic soil in gardens, parkland or heatland végétation $ G. obscuroides
